    {
      "category": "craft-fairs",
      "label": "Craft Fairs & Markets",
      "description": "Makers' markets, Christmas craft fairs, and the weekly country markets that carry craft stalls alongside the food.\nStrongly seasonal: a handful of dates through the summer, then everything at once from late October to Christmas. The weekly markets are `recurring` rather than fifty-two events — Skibbereen on Saturday, Bantry on Friday, Clonakilty on Friday — and the thing worth catching is the special fair that lands on top of one of them.",
      "sourceCount": 3,
      "eventCount": 1
    },
    {
      "category": "galleries",
      "label": "Galleries, Studios & Heritage",
      "description": "Places with a room and a programme: the region's one substantial arts centre, and the heritage centres and museums that put on exhibitions, talks and open days.\nKept because exhibitions of local making are in scope and because these are the only sources here that publish a proper diary. They are the easy half of the catalogue; everything hard is in `notices`.",
      "sourceCount": 4,
      "eventCount": 6
    },
    {
      "category": "notices",
      "label": "Local Notices & Listings",
      "description": "The local papers, the regional tourism site, the island community pages and the national heritage schemes.\n**In a city this would be the safety net. Here it is the main net.** A great deal of what this location exists to catch happens in a parish hall, a community centre or somebody's studio, and the only published trace is a diary column or a scheme's listing. Both of the events that started this — a flax and linen day in Clonakilty, a Shetland knitting weekend on Cape Clear — were that kind of event.\nEverything here needs filtering hard on the way in. Most of what these sources carry is a gig guide, a match report or a festival programme, none of which belongs in this location. Take the making strand and leave the rest.",
      "sourceCount": 6,
      "eventCount": 0
    },
    {
      "category": "textiles",
      "label": "Textiles & Fibre",
      "description": "Wool, knitting, weaving, spinning, dyeing, felting, stitch — and flax and linen, which West Cork grew and processed until the industry collapsed two centuries ago and is now reviving.\nThis is the anchor category and the thinnest one, which is the finding rather than a gap. Fibre in West Cork is organised by people, not by venues: a network, a heritage society, a handful of makers, and a Facebook page each. Almost none of it has a listings page, so most of what belongs here will be caught by `notices` and only later attributed back to whoever ran it.\nBoth anchor events sit here. The Clonakilty flax day is Dúchas Clonakilty Heritage's, and it does have a site. The Cape Clear knitting weekend has no standing home at all and has to be caught through the island's own page or the local papers.",
      "sourceCount": 2,
      "eventCount": 5
    },
    {
      "category": "workshops",
      "label": "Workshops & Courses",
      "description": "Hands-on making you can turn up and do: day classes, weekend courses, summer schools, artist-led sessions.\nThe distinction from `craft-fairs` is whether you go to buy or to make. A weekend of Shetland knitting on an island belongs here; a Christmas fair does not.",
      "sourceCount": 2,
      "eventCount": 0
    }
  ],
